// A value with no runtime presence (docs/language/ownership-design.md
// §10.6 to §10.8, §13): a generation, a region's token, a key's version.
// It is part of what a function means and of what its theorems state, and
// a `runtime fn` that takes one as a parameter is compiled to code that
// does not (issues/runtime-erased-parameters.md): the reflector drops the
// parameter, the generator states every theorem of the function at each
// value of it, and a body that computes with it (reads `g.value` into a
// result, branches on it) is refused by name, since the code has nothing
// to compute from.
//
// Why a wrapper type and not a mark on the binder: a type needs no parser,
// reaches every shape of `runtime fn` through the one place the reflector
// reads a parameter's type, and travels with the value into callers and
// records. A proof needs no wrapper: a parameter whose type is a
// proposition (`∥P∥`, any two of whose inhabitants are one term) is erased
// the same way. `Erased<A>` is for data, whose inhabitants differ, so the
// meaning may depend on it where the code may not.
//
// Nothing makes `Erased<A>` opaque to proofs: `g.value` is an ordinary
// field, and a theorem about a function that takes one reads it freely.
record Erased<A> { value: A }

// The value a function threads unchanged, as erased state is: a method
// that keeps its generation returns the one it was given.
theorem Erased::eta<A>(g: Erased<A>): Erased(g.value) = g {
    match g { Erased(_) => refl }
}
